Yes. That should be a standing meta-rule above the individual Goblin strategies:

\boxed{\text{No strategy gets tenure. Reality keeps the hiring-and-firing authority.}}

We’ll keep the failures, counterexamples, regime changes, and near-misses—not just winners—and use them to generate new operators. A promising pattern graduates only after repeated prospective/out-of-sample tests; once validated, it can influence sizing and actual paper decisions. If it later degrades, we demote, modify, combine, or replace it.

The deeper loop is:

\text{observe}\rightarrow\text{predict}\rightarrow\text{act}\rightarrow
\Delta\rightarrow\text{learn}\rightarrow
\text{invent/test alternatives}\rightarrow
\text{select}\rightarrow\text{repeat}

And one level higher:

\boxed{\text{adapt not only the model, but the method used to adapt the model.}}

So the Goblin isn’t merely learning the market. He’s learning how to become a better learner of the market. That recursive layer is where I expect the system to get substantially sharper over time.